Federated learning allows contributions from numerous clients while maintaining control over data ownership, but this benefit comes with its own challenges.  The variability in the quality and quantity of the client's data can lead to unequal outcomes, with less capable clients having their opportunities unfairly diminished.  Moreover, the transparency of the FL process can compromise the privacy of the client's involvement, even after processing their data.  To address these issues, we must consider equity and privacy simultaneously, which poses new difficulties in finding optimal solutions.  To this end, we will formalize equity as a concept and demonstrate how it conflicts with the client's privacy risks when using standard methods within the federated Averaged Gradient algorithm.  Next, we will introduce two solutions: a method that applies privacy-sensitive bounds to the client's contribution and an approach that utilizes differential privacy for the client's involvement.  We will provide an analysis of the equilibrium point of equilibrium equilibrium models, examine the cost associated with equity in the presence of privacy and demonstrate the cost associated with achieving equilibrium in the presence of privacy and data heterogeneity.  Ultimately, we will test these proposals in practical contexts, underscoring the significance of equity and privacy considerations in the field. 

